{"id":"bonus-or-raise-in-a-hard-year","title":"A bonus or a raise in a hard year","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Where the City is a main funder, which organizations paid bonuses, or raised their top officer’s pay by a tenth or more, in a year when they ran a deficit, cut staff, carried federal audit findings or fell short with a City monitor?","finding":"Across 1,029 organization-years in which City payments were a quarter or more of revenue, 68 at 54 organizations combine a bonus or a raise of a tenth or more for the top officer with at least one measurable difficulty in the same year: a deficit of 2% of spending or more (51), employees down by a tenth or more (23), federal audit findings (6) or a programme below target with a City monitor (2); 14 show two or more. 22 involve bonuses and 48 a raise. Of 360 top-officer pay changes that could be tested, 121 were rises of a tenth or more. First on the list: Main Street Launch, year ended 30 June 2024 (spent $3.1 million more than it took in; employees down from 20 to 17). A further 17 bonus or raise years are at organizations among the 60 most-paid for which no result recorded by anyone else was found; they are listed last.","how":"Tax returns covering eleven months or more in which City payments were at least 25% of revenue. Bonuses are from the compensation schedule, from the organization’s own funds, counted where they come to $10,000 or more between all listed people. The top officer is the best-paid current officer whose title says chief executive, executive director or president; a raise compares that person’s total pay (from the organization and related organizations) with the year before, and is tested only where the person was already listed with pay two returns back, so that the earlier figure is a full year. The marks: revenue less expenses below zero by 2% of spending or more; the employee count on the return down by a tenth or more on the previous return (ten or more employees); a federal audit with findings for the year (from the official-record pages); a Public Health or homelessness contract monitor recording a programme below standard or under 80% of contracted objectives for the City fiscal year that the tax year mostly covers (from the results pages); and, for the 60 organizations paid most, a scorecard verdict of “only its own reports” or “no result on record”, which is not tied to a year. Ranked by the number of year-specific marks, then by the money. Where the organization’s tax number was matched by hand and has not been verified, the last column says so.","limits":"None of these combinations is improper in itself. A deficit can be planned spending of reserves or a grant booked in another year; staff counts fall when a programme ends; an audit finding says a requirement or control was not met, and most are fixed; a monitoring shortfall concerns one programme of many and several fall in pandemic years. A bonus may have been earned the year before or owed under contract, and a raise may follow a promotion or a market review; pay on the return is for the calendar year ending inside the tax year. The monitoring list on disk holds the strongest 96 of 96 provider-years, so some shortfalls are missed. The pattern is a reason to ask what the board tied the payment to, nothing more.","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"year","t":"Tax year ended","type":"date"},{"k":"marks","t":"What the same year shows","type":"text"},{"k":"bonuses","t":"Bonuses to listed people","type":"usd"},{"k":"bonusPeople","t":"People","type":"int"},{"k":"topBonusTo","t":"Largest bonus to","type":"person"},{"k":"topBonus","t":"Largest bonus","type":"usd"},{"k":"officer","t":"Top officer with a raise","type":"person"},{"k":"payBefore","t":"Pay the year before","type":"usd"},{"k":"payNow","t":"Pay that year","type":"usd"},{"k":"raised","t":"Raise","type":"pct"},{"k":"surplus","t":"Revenue less expenses","type":"usd"},{"k":"staffChange","t":"Change in employees","type":"p